A sink that provably discards what it is handed was refused on the
io-boundary claim path and accepted on the taint path. One repository
holding one script whose only redirect is `echo "$API_KEY" > /dev/null`,
verified twice against the same tree with the map pinned by --input:

  {boundary: fs_write, must_not_exist: true}      confirmed  rc 0
  {taint_flow: host_secret -> host_fs}            violated   rc 1

Same tree, same edge, opposite verdicts. INV-nular wired
target_kinds_cross_no_boundary into tag_io_boundaries; the taint arm
derives its sinks straight from the catalogue and never saw the
per-call-site io_target_kind.

The clause lands in _sink_call_can_carry_taint, the one predicate both
propagators already share, so it cannot be true of one arm and false of
the other. Refusing further upstream in classify_call_in_catalog is not
repeated -- INV-nular measured that worse, because the coverage gate asks
"did the catalogue examine this call" and the answer is yes.

One gate, one gap, established by enumeration: the boundary path reads
call_site_modes and call_site_target_kinds; the taint path reads
call_site_modes and call_arg_shape. Only target kinds were missing.

Measured paired through the production CLI with the discarding vocabulary
emptied to reproduce the pre-fix path (canary asserts the arms differ):
cert-manager 37 -> 30 flows, spacedrive 81 -> 74, guacamole-client 12 -> 11.
130 -> 115 overall, evidence rows 26 -> 14. Every removed row read back
against source targets /dev/null; no real write was silenced.

The source side is deliberately ungated -- no boundary carrying an
io_target_kind stamp derives a taint source, so the clause would be
unreachable -- and a test re-derives that premise from every shipped
catalogue rather than asserting it in a comment.

